        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8052/0 Squamous papilloma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137310,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F36.Y &amp; XH50T2 Other specified benign neoplasm of eye or ocular adnexa &amp; Squamous cell papilloma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137311,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> skin tag; squamous cell papilloma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> acrochordon.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137312,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137313,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions can be anywhere on the skin, including the eyelid.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137314,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squamous papilloma is a sessile or pedunculated soft, smooth lesion with a similar colour to adjacent skin. The surface is more-or-less verrucous, with or without a keratin crust. Ulceration due to mechanical irritation may be present. Papillomas are often multiple {19384066}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137315,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squamous papilloma is among the most common benign eyelid tumours and can occur at any age, with no sex predilection {30450316;26634208;34849127}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137316,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Some lesions are HPV-related and may represent burned-out HPV infections {32809572}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137317,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Low-risk HPV types induce benign neoplastic epithelial proliferation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137318,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squamous papilloma is a sessile or pedunculated, small, skin-coloured lesion. The surface is more-or-less verrucous, with or without a keratin crust. It is sometimes ulcerated.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137319,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 48,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squamous papilloma is a verrucous, broad-based or finger-like exophytic epithelial neoplasm with a fibrovascular core. There is an absence of dysplasia and of relevant mitotic activity, with no signs of dermal invasion. Hyperparakeratosis or ulceration due to mechanical irritation may be present. In contrast to verruca vulgaris, squamous cell papillomas have an exophytic, papillomatous, and less acanthomatous growth pattern. HPV cytopathic signs, such as koilocytes or double nuclei, may occur. However, some squamous cell papillomas may represent regressed verruca vulgaris.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:58:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
